%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 835431524041ce44e889351e0ffe7924, type: 3}
  m_Name: Carlos_Victim
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  entryID: Carlos
  isScammer: 0
  steps:
  - npcLine: I just got back from that Aikido seminar and posted a bunch of photos
      of our training and the restaurant we went to afterward. Then, an hour later,
      I got a call from 'Security' at that exact restaurant!
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: That timing is too perfect to be a coincidence.
      How did they know where he was?'
    choices:
    - text: They likely saw your public 'check-in' or photos on social media.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: They must have been following you physically the entire day.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: The restaurant must have a policy of calling every customer after they
        leave.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  - npcLine: The caller said I left my wallet there and they need to 'verify my identity'
      by having me confirm my home address and birthday before they mail it back.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: This is a classic social engineering setup. They
      use a helpful scenario to extract data.'
    choices:
    - text: This is likely a scam script designed to harvest your personal details
        for identity theft.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: It sounds like a very nice gesture from the restaurant staff.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -10
    - text: I'll tell them to just keep the wallet as a tip.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  - npcLine: But wait, how did they get my phone number? I didn't post that on social
      media.
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Once they have your name and location from your
      post, finding your number is the easy part.'
    choices:
    - text: Scammers combine social media details with data broker sites to find
        your contact info.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: They probably got it from your gym's membership list.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
    - text: Your phone must be broadcasting your number to everyone nearby via Bluetooth.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
  - npcLine: He even mentioned my 'Unity 2D game project' because he saw the laptop
      stickers in the restaurant. He sounded like a fellow developer!
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Building rapport by finding common interests
      is a key social engineering tactic.'
    choices:
    - text: He's using 'affinity' to lower your guard. Don't let shared hobbies cloud
        your judgment.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: "If he\u2019s a developer, he\u2019s probably one of the good guys."
      type: bad
      pointValue: -15
    - text: Ask him what version of Unity he uses to see if he's lying.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 10
  - npcLine: "He\u2019s asking for my social security number to 'match it with the
      ID in the wallet.' Is that standard procedure?"
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Absolutely not. No restaurant or even the police
      would ask for that over the phone.'
    choices:
    - text: Never provide your SSN over the phone to an unsolicited caller, regardless
        of the story.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: Give it to him. You really need that wallet back for your trip tomorrow.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -20
    - text: Ask him to read the SSN on the ID to you first.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
  - npcLine: "I told him I\u2019d just drive back to the restaurant and pick it up
      in person. He suddenly got very nervous and said they already 'sent it to the
      central processing center.'"
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: When the scammer loses control of the situation,
      they often invent new hurdles or urgency.'
    choices:
    - text: The change in his tone and the 'processing center' excuse are massive
        red flags.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: He's probably just worried about the paperwork for a lost item.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -10
    - text: Tell him you'll meet him at the 'central processing center' instead.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
  - npcLine: I just checked my pocket... my wallet is right here! He was lying the
      whole time!
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: The ''lost item'' lure is powerful because it
      triggers immediate anxiety and a desire to cooperate.'
    choices:
    - text: This proves that scammers use your real-time social media activity to
        create 'personalized' lies.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: Maybe you have two wallets and you lost the other one?
      type: bad
      pointValue: -20
    - text: He must have been a magician who put the wallet back in your pocket.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -25
  - npcLine: I'm looking at my social media settings. My profile is set to 'Public.'
      Should I change that?
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Limiting who can see your activity is the best
      way to prevent this kind of targeting.'
    choices:
    - text: Yes, set your profile to 'Private' and be selective about who you add
        as a friend.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: No, just keep posting but never mention where you are until you leave.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
    - text: Just delete all your photos. Text-only posts are much safer.
      type: bad
      pointValue: -10
  - npcLine: "I\u2019ve learned my lesson. No more 'real-time' check-ins or public
      photos of my daily routine."
    assistantLine: 'Internal Voice: Privacy habits are your best defense against
      personalized social engineering.'
    choices:
    - text: I'll wait until I'm home to post photos from my Aikido seminars from
        now on.
      type: good
      pointValue: 15
    - text: I'll just use a fake name on my social media from now on.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 5
    - text: I'm going to post a fake photo of me at the airport to trick the scammers.
      type: tossup
      pointValue: 0
  successEnding: Correct! You recognized that oversharing on social media allows
    scammers to craft highly convincing, personalized scripts, making their vishing
    attempts much harder to detect.
  failureEnding: "Incorrect. You fell for the 'lost wallet' lure because the scammer\u2019s
    use of real-time details made them seem legitimate, leading you to almost give
    up your SSN."
